$DIR/main.p:16:10: the field rows.data[i] of a synced[DB] is read here without holding mu, which guards it
    Every write to the field rows.data[i] of a synced[DB] that another task may
    run at the same time holds mu: pluto/vec/vec.p:152 in (*Vec).Push (the task
    started by task writer at main.p:34), pluto/vec/vec.p:434 in (*Vec).realloc
    (the task started by task writer at main.p:34), pluto/vec/vec.p:446 in
    (*Vec).free (the task started by task writer at main.p:34). So the compiler
    infers that mu guards the field rows.data[i] of a synced[DB], and every read
    must hold it too: a read without it may run while a write is half done.
    Here the task started by task reader at main.p:33 reads it at main.p:16 in
    reader with no lock held.
    Go leaves data races to a run-time detector that a program must be run
    under, and a racing Go program can see values half written. Plutonium tasks
    run in parallel with no such detector, so safe code must be free of data
    races, and the compiler checks it from the program itself, with no
    annotations.
    Hold the lock while reading:
        mu.Lock()
        v := the field rows.data[i] of a synced[DB]
        mu.Unlock()
    (see "Data race freedom" in the Plutonium spec, section 19.3)
    (run "pluto explain E3503" for a longer explanation)
